= Prime number =

== Overview ==
A '''prime number''' is an integer greater than 1 whose only positive integer divisors are 1 and itself.

== Definition ==
Examples are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11. An integer greater than 1 that is not prime is composite.

== Scope & importance ==
Prime numbers are fundamental building blocks of positive integers. The fundamental theorem of arithmetic states that every integer greater than 1 can be expressed as a product of prime numbers uniquely up to the order of the factors.

== Core concepts ==
Primality concerns divisibility. The number 1 is neither prime nor composite. The number 2 is the only even prime because every other even integer greater than 2 is divisible by 2.

== Examples ==
13 is prime because its positive divisors are only 1 and 13. 15 is composite because 15 = 3 × 5.

== Applications / implications ==
Prime numbers are central to number theory and appear in algorithms, primality testing, factorization, and many cryptographic constructions.

== Epistemic notes ==
The definition of primality is elementary, while the distribution and behavior of primes lead to deep open problems. A simple definition can therefore support a very large theoretical structure.

